WEBVTT
NOTE FoundOS Chapter 3 V2 review captions. DRAFT — NOT APPROVED FOR PUBLIC USE.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.249
Now, after looking at your customer's
journey, let's say you noticed something

2
00:00:03.289 --> 00:00:04.344
that could be easier.

3
00:00:04.924 --> 00:00:06.460
What should you actually do about it?

4
00:00:07.084 --> 00:00:11.272
I don't think the answer should be
automatically a new website, an ad

5
00:00:11.312 --> 00:00:13.216
campaign, or a new AI tool.

6
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.524
It depends on what needs attention.

7
00:00:15.729 --> 00:00:17.282
Let me show you what I mean.

8
00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:20.759
Imagine you offered three
different service packages.

9
00:00:21.184 --> 00:00:23.787
You understand exactly
what's included in each one.

10
00:00:24.005 --> 00:00:26.839
But someone visiting your website
for the first time might not.

11
00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.891
They might be wondering which
package fits their needs,

12
00:00:30.356 --> 00:00:31.573
what they're paying for,

13
00:00:31.794 --> 00:00:34.175
or whether they need to
contact you to get an answer.

14
00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:36.740
Maybe the improvement is simple.

15
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.162
Clear descriptions,

16
00:00:38.427 --> 00:00:40.690
easier comparison between the services,

17
00:00:40.938 --> 00:00:42.818
a better explanation of what happens next.

18
00:00:43.491 --> 00:00:46.812
Sometimes the first improvement is just
helping your customer understand what

19
00:00:46.852 --> 00:00:47.852
you're offering.

20
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.221
Now let's say you're promoting one of your
services through an Instagram or Facebook

21
00:00:52.261 --> 00:00:53.040
ad.

22
00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.237
Someone sees the ad and gets interested.

23
00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:56.510
They click.

24
00:00:56.785 --> 00:00:59.749
But instead of finding more
information about the specific service,

25
00:00:59.948 --> 00:01:01.968
they land on the general homepage

26
00:01:02.252 --> 00:01:04.448
and have to search for the
service that you mentioned.

27
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.358
What if the page they landed on answers
the questions that brought them there in

28
00:01:08.398 --> 00:01:09.398
the first place?

29
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.686
What the service includes,

30
00:01:11.806 --> 00:01:12.806
who it's for,

31
00:01:13.076 --> 00:01:14.691
and how to take that next step.

32
00:01:15.364 --> 00:01:18.924
The message before the click should
connect the experience after the click.

33
00:01:19.926 --> 00:01:23.264
That doesn't guarantee someone will buy,
but it makes their journey easier to

34
00:01:23.304 --> 00:01:24.304
understand.

35
00:01:25.033 --> 00:01:27.838
Now imagine your customer is
ready to book or reach out.

36
00:01:28.374 --> 00:01:29.821
They already want to talk to you.

37
00:01:29.946 --> 00:01:31.093
The question becomes...

38
00:01:31.204 --> 00:01:33.002
how easy have you made that?

39
00:01:33.196 --> 00:01:35.832
Maybe the booking button
can be a little bit clearer.

40
00:01:36.063 --> 00:01:38.719
Maybe the form could ask fewer
questions at the beginning.

41
00:01:38.988 --> 00:01:41.817
Maybe the confirmation could
explain what they might expect.

42
00:01:42.905 --> 00:01:45.632
I'm not talking about pressuring
someone to make a decision.

43
00:01:45.775 --> 00:01:49.206
I'm talking about removing unnecessary
effort for someone who's already

44
00:01:49.246 --> 00:01:50.246
interested.

45
00:01:50.858 --> 00:01:53.583
Make the next step clear
and let the customer decide.

46
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.520
And what about after
they send that message?

47
00:01:57.016 --> 00:02:00.498
Suppose you're busy working with a
customer and can't respond immediately.

48
00:02:01.026 --> 00:02:05.434
A useful automated acknowledgement might
confirm that the message was received,

49
00:02:05.708 --> 00:02:07.075
explain what happens next,

50
00:02:07.505 --> 00:02:09.937
and show how to reach a
real person if needed.

51
00:02:10.632 --> 00:02:14.009
But I wouldn't recommend automating
something before understanding how your

52
00:02:14.049 --> 00:02:15.939
business actually handles inquiries.

53
00:02:16.945 --> 00:02:20.454
And I wouldn't want a customer to think
they're speaking to a real person when

54
00:02:20.494 --> 00:02:22.973
they're actually receiving
an automated response.

55
00:02:23.506 --> 00:02:27.126
Technology should help you take care of
the people, not get in between you and

56
00:02:27.166 --> 00:02:28.166
them.

57
00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:31.417
Sometimes the opportunity is
after someone has already booked.

58
00:02:32.132 --> 00:02:34.824
Maybe your customer needs
clearer appointment instructions.

59
00:02:35.131 --> 00:02:38.149
Maybe they need to know how to
contact someone if something changes.

60
00:02:38.574 --> 00:02:42.441
Or maybe it should be easier to ask a
question or return for another service.

61
00:02:42.974 --> 00:02:45.196
These might seem like small details.

62
00:02:45.451 --> 00:02:46.451
But to me,

63
00:02:46.677 --> 00:02:49.843
they're part of showing someone you care
about the whole experience, and not just

64
00:02:49.883 --> 00:02:50.924
getting the booking.

65
00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:53.351
Now I want you to remember something.

66
00:02:53.713 --> 00:02:56.388
Your business might not need
any of those exact changes.

67
00:02:56.777 --> 00:03:00.908
What works for a detailing shop might be
different from what works for a salon,

68
00:03:00.948 --> 00:03:03.310
restaurant, or another service business.

69
00:03:04.165 --> 00:03:07.970
That's why I believe you should understand
the problem before choosing a solution.

70
00:03:08.574 --> 00:03:10.053
And if you do make that change,

71
00:03:10.226 --> 00:03:12.027
it's worth asking whether it helped.

72
00:03:12.834 --> 00:03:14.733
What can you measure before the change?

73
00:03:15.007 --> 00:03:16.308
What happens afterward?

74
00:03:16.581 --> 00:03:18.263
And what else might
explain the difference?

75
00:03:19.228 --> 00:03:23.156
I don't want FoundOS to be about selling
you technology you might not need.

76
00:03:23.621 --> 00:03:26.349
I want it to be about
understanding your business,

77
00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.455
identifying practical improvements,

78
00:03:28.865 --> 00:03:31.339
and helping you create a better
experience for your customers.

79
00:03:31.881 --> 00:03:33.099
So the next question is...

80
00:03:33.431 --> 00:03:34.431
where should you start?

81
00:03:34.494 --> 00:03:37.043
That's what I want to explain
in this final chapter.
